var AQ=[
['ps','A shirt’s price goes up by 20%, then down by 20%. What is the overall change?',['No change','4% decrease','4% increase','2% decrease'],1,'1.2 × 0.8 = 0.96, so the final price is 96% of the start: a 4% fall.'],
['ps','Boys and girls in a class are in the ratio 3 : 5. After 12 more boys join, the ratio is 1 : 1. How many girls are there?',['24','30','36','40'],1,'3k + 12 = 5k, so k = 6 and girls = 5 × 6 = 30.'],
['ps','If 2x + 3 = 11 and y = x² − 1, what is y?',['15','16','17','9'],0,'x = 4, so y = 16 − 1 = 15.'],
['ps','A train covers 180 km in 2.5 hours. What is its speed in metres per second?',['20','25','18','72'],0,'180 ÷ 2.5 = 72 km/h, and 72 × 5/18 = 20 m/s.'],
['ps','The average of five numbers is 18. One is removed and the average of the other four is 16. What was removed?',['24','26','28','2'],1,'5 × 18 = 90 and 4 × 16 = 64, so 90 − 64 = 26.'],
['ps','What comes next: 2, 6, 12, 20, 30, …?',['40','42','44','36'],1,'Each term is n(n + 1): 6 × 7 = 42. The gaps 4, 6, 8, 10, 12 show it too.'],
['ps','A cube painted on every face is cut into 27 equal small cubes. How many small cubes have exactly two painted faces?',['8','12','6','1'],1,'Two painted faces sit on the edges, one middle cube per edge: 12 edges give 12.'],
['ps','A shop sold 40 books on Monday, 25 on Tuesday and 35 on Wednesday. What percentage of the three-day total was sold on Tuesday?',['20%','25%','30%','35%'],1,'25 out of 100 books is 25%.'],
['ps','A clock shows 3:15. What is the angle between the hour and minute hands?',['0°','7.5°','15°','22.5°'],1,'The minute hand is at 90°. The hour hand has moved a quarter of 30° past 3, so it is at 97.5°. The gap is 7.5°.'],
['ps','A square sheet is folded in half, then in half again, and one hole is punched through all the layers. How many holes are there when it is unfolded?',['2','3','4','8'],2,'Two folds make 4 layers, so one punch makes 4 holes.'],
['ct','Every member of the debate club reads the newspaper. Ria reads the newspaper. Which statement must be true?',['Ria is in the debate club','Ria may or may not be in the debate club','Ria is not in the debate club','Every newspaper reader is in the club'],1,'Club → reader does not give reader → club. Ria could be a reader outside the club.'],
['ct','“Umbrella sales rose this month, so it must have rained more.” Which assumption does this rely on?',['Umbrella prices fell','Nothing other than more rain explains the higher sales','Umbrellas are cheap','It rained last month'],1,'The conclusion only follows if no other cause (a sale, a new shop, sun protection) is behind the rise.'],
['ct','“Students who sleep eight hours score higher, so schools should start later.” Which fact weakens this most?',['Many students like late starts','Where schools started later, students slept no more; they just went to bed later','Sleep is healthy','Exams are held in the morning'],1,'If a later start does not add sleep, the plan cannot deliver the benefit the argument relies on.'],
['ct','“The city’s new cycle lanes have reduced traffic jams.” Which fact strengthens this most?',['Cycle-lane use rose sharply while car counts fell on those roads','Cycles are cheaper than cars','Some people dislike cycling','Fuel prices rose in the same months'],0,'It links the lanes to fewer cars. The fuel-price option is a rival cause and would weaken it.'],
['ct','“My grandfather smoked and lived to 95, so smoking is not harmful.” What is the main flaw?',['It uses one case to reject a general finding','It uses technical terms','It attacks a person','It assumes the future repeats the past'],0,'One exception does not overturn evidence from millions of people.'],
['ct','All the paintings in Room A are watercolours. Some watercolours in the museum are by Indian artists. What follows?',['Some Room A paintings are by Indian artists','No Room A painting is by an Indian artist','Nothing follows about who painted the Room A works','All watercolours are in Room A'],2,'The Indian-artist watercolours could be in other rooms, so neither yes nor no is forced.'],
['ct','Principle: a promise should be kept unless keeping it would cause serious harm to someone. Which action fits it?',['Missing a meeting with a friend to take an injured stranger to hospital','Breaking a promise because you feel tired','Keeping a promise to share an answer key during an exam','Breaking a promise because a better party came up'],0,'Only the first breaks a promise to prevent serious harm. Keeping a harmful promise (the answer key) goes against the principle.'],
['ct','“If it is a weekday, the library is open. The library is closed. So it is not a weekday.” Which has the same structure?',['If Raj practises, he wins. Raj did not win, so he did not practise.','If Raj practises, he wins. Raj won, so he practised.','Raj practises, so he wins.','If Raj wins, he practised. Raj practised.'],0,'If P then Q; not Q; so not P. Option B is the flawed reverse.'],
['ct','“Online classes saved travel time. But students reported less focus and more screen fatigue, and teachers found it harder to spot confusion.” What is the main point?',['Online classes carry real costs alongside the time they save','Online classes are better','Teachers dislike technology','Travel is the main issue'],0,'The “but” sets the costs against the one benefit.'],
['ct','“A survey of 50 people at a gym found 90% exercise weekly. So most Indians exercise weekly.” What is the flaw?',['The sample does not represent all Indians','50 is an even number','The survey was not online','Gyms are expensive'],0,'People at a gym are the group most likely to exercise, so they cannot stand for the whole country.']];
